    "summary": "Compare local in-process tools versus out-of-process MCP servers across 9 architectural dimensions, and navigate the 'Build Local First, Migrate When Justified' decision path.",
    "content": "### 1. What are Local Tools vs. MCP Servers? (The Definitive Definition)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Formal Technical Definition (Exam Standard):**  \n> **Local Tools** are in-process Python callable functions executed directly within the agent application's memory space. **MCP Servers** are decoupled, out-of-process standalone services communicating over standardized JSON-RPC protocols, providing fault isolation and multi-client reusability at the cost of inter-process serialization latency.\n\n#### \u2696\ufe0f The 9-Dimension Comparison Matrix (Exam Reference)\n\n| Dimension | Local In-Process Tools | MCP Out-of-Process Servers |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1. Execution Boundary** | In-process Python function call in local RAM. | Out-of-process service over `stdio` or `HTTP/SSE`. |\n| **2. Invocation Latency** | **Sub-millisecond** (instantaneous memory pointer). | Low ($\\sim 2-10\text{ms}$ over stdio; higher over network). |\n| **3. Fault Isolation** | **None:** A memory leak or crash can kill the agent. | **Complete:** A server crash is isolated; host survives. |\n| **4. Security Boundary** | Shared memory and credentials with host. | Strict boundary; scoped credentials per server. |\n| **5. Language Independence**| Locked to the agent's language (e.g. Python). | Polyglot: Host in Python, server in TypeScript or Rust. |\n| **6. Reusability** | Locked to a single codebase. | **Universal:** Shared across Claude Desktop, Cursor, etc. |\n| **7. Tool Discovery** | Hardcoded in Python code. | Dynamic runtime discovery via `tools/list`. |\n| **8. Deployment & Ops** | Trivial (no extra processes to manage). | Requires managing separate server processes/containers. |\n| **9. Best Use Case** | Private scripts, rapid prototypes, fast math. | Enterprise shared tools, multi-app environments. |\n\n---\n\n### 2. Decision Path: \"Build Local First, Migrate When Justified\"\n\n```text\nTHE ARCHITECTURAL DECISION PATH\nDoes this tool need to be shared across multiple IDEs/apps, or require fault-isolated privileges?\n                       \u2502\n             \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n          NO \u2502                   \u2502 YES\n             \u25bc                   \u25bc\n    [ Keep Tool Local ]    [ Deploy MCP Server ]\n    \u2022 Simple Python fn     \u2022 FastMCP over stdio / SSE\n    \u2022 Zero deployment ops  \u2022 Sandboxed process boundary\n    \u2022 Sub-ms latency       \u2022 Universal multi-client reuse\n```\n\n---\n\n### \u26a1 2-Minute Exam Quick Review Cheat Sheet\n\n* **Local Tool:** In-process, fastest performance (sub-ms), but zero fault isolation and locked to one codebase.\n* **MCP Server:** Out-of-process JSON-RPC, complete fault isolation, language agnostic, universally reusable across apps.\n* **Fault Isolation Advantage:** If an MCP tool crashes, the host agent process stays completely alive and recovers.\n* **Golden Rule:** Build local first for simplicity; migrate to MCP when tool reuse or security isolation is justified."
